  "common_pitfalls": [
    "Proving only existential bounds ('multi-vector CAN represent X') without tight characterization of the gap. Wang et al.'s R^{2k} result succeeds because it is tight (Θ(k)); a loose 'multi-vector needs at most O(k²d²) dimension' would be seen as incremental. The baseline fear test from the research direction is accurate: proving 'multi-vector is at least as expressive' is trivial and publishable nowhere.",
    "Conflating approximation guarantees with expressiveness characterization. MUVERA proves ε-approximation for Chamfer→MIPS reduction but this says nothing about what relevance patterns MaxSim can express that inner product cannot. Several arXiv preprints have made this error — proving algorithmic reductions are good approximations and claiming this addresses expressiveness.",
    "Ignoring computational feasibility of theoretically-optimal representations. The α-reachable graphs paper (ICML 2025) shows that Chamfer search guarantees degrade with quasimetric constant k, creating tension between richer representations and search efficiency. Bounds requiring k = Ω(n) vectors per document are theoretically clean but computationally vacuous — reviewers at AAAI will reject these as impractical.",
    "Using only toy stochastic models without formal worst-case bounds. Jacob et al. (arXiv 2026) uses i.i.d. unit vectors on spheres and derives suggestive exp(-Θ(d/log n)) vs exp(-Θ(d/n)) separation, but the paper explicitly acknowledges this is 'not a formal expressiveness bound' — and remains on arXiv rather than at a top venue. Stochastic models are useful for intuition but reviewers demand worst-case or distributional-worst-case results.",
