    "Presenting communication complexity results in notation inaccessible to the ML/IR community. The Z2-topological framework paper uses heavy algebraic topology (Vietoris-Rips complexes, nerve lemma, Z2-coindex) that is natural for STOC/FOCS but alienating at AAAI. Successful cross-pollination papers (Weller et al., Wang et al.) use elementary geometric arguments. If the core insight requires topological machinery, invest in making the intuition accessible via geometric illustrations and worked examples.",
    "Neglecting the dimension-vs-vectors tradeoff curve. A common mistake would be proving that k vectors in R^d suffice for some task without explicitly characterizing the Pareto frontier: for each total parameter budget kd, what is the optimal split between k and d? This is the practically actionable question that system builders need answered.",
    "Ignoring the asymmetry between query and document representations. MaxSim scoring in ColBERT uses different numbers of vectors for queries vs documents, and MUVERA exploits asymmetric encoding. Theoretical frameworks that assume symmetric k-vs-k representation miss practical deployment realities where documents have variable-length token sequences."
  ],
  "field_trajectory": "The field is converging on three macro-directions. First, a complete expressiveness hierarchy for retrieval scoring functions is being assembled piece by piece: dot product capacity is now well-characterized via sign-rank (Weller et al.) and MED (Wang et al.), MoL has been shown to be a universal approximator (RAILS, WWW 2025), and cross-encoders are trivially full-rank. The critical missing piece is precisely where MaxSim/multi-vector sits in this hierarchy, and the field is clearly aware of and hungry for this result — Jacob et al., Weller et al., and Wang et al. all explicitly identify it as the key open question. Second, there is a push toward unified theories that connect architectural choices to both expressiveness AND computational efficiency. The α-reachable graphs paper (ICML 2025), WARP (SIGIR 2025), and the Chamfer distance algorithm (ICALP 2025) show that the systems and theory communities are converging: expressiveness bounds that ignore search complexity will increasingly seem incomplete. Third, the mechanistic interpretability direction (superposition, ETFs, succinctness) is beginning to explain WHY learned representations hit capacity limits rather than just proving they exist. The 2026-2027 horizon will likely see these three directions merge: a paper that formally characterizes MaxSim expressiveness, connects it to efficient search guarantees, and provides mechanistic intuition for when and why multi-vector representations are learned would be field-defining.",
  "meta_advice": "Target the exact gap that four independent groups have identified but none have filled: formal expressiveness bounds for MaxSim as a function of (k, d). Your strongest strategy is to define a clean complexity measure — call it 'MaxSim-rank' or 'k-sign-rank' — that reduces to classical sign-rank when k=1 and provably decreases as k grows, then prove tight bounds on this measure for specific matrix families (especially the LIMIT relevance matrix and Gap Hamming Distance matrices where sign-rank bounds are known). Use elementary geometric arguments accessible to AAAI reviewers rather than heavy topology, even if topology gives stronger bounds — save the Z2-complex extension for a STOC/FOCS companion paper. Structure the paper as: (1) define k-sign-rank via multi-message communication protocol, (2) prove k-sign-rank ≤ sign-rank with explicit gap for k ≥ 2, (3) construct explicit matrix families where the gap is exponential, (4) prove the dimension-vectors tradeoff theorem showing the Pareto frontier of kd budget, (5) validate on LIMIT using free multi-vector embeddings and show that theoretical predictions match empirical k-vs-recall curves. The single most important thing to get right is the separation result: you need at least one concrete relevance matrix family where single-vector provably requires dimension Ω(f(n)) but multi-vector with k vectors needs only O(f(n)/g(k)) for some g growing with k. Without this, the paper is a formalism paper, not a results paper. The Gap Hamming Distance matrices are your best candidate because sign-rank lower bounds exist (Z2-topological framework achieves ~2k) and the combinatorial structure may allow k vectors to cover different Hamming shells independently. Budget your 200 H100-hours for the LIMIT free-embedding optimization experiments (sweep k ∈ {1,2,4,8,16,32} at d ∈ {32,64,128,256}) and for training ColBERT variants with controlled vector counts to validate that theoretical capacity improvements translate to learned model performance. Submit to AAAI's Information Retrieval track rather than ML Theory — the IR reviewers are more receptive to results that explain retrieval phenomena, while ML Theory reviewers may demand tighter bounds or broader scope than a first paper can deliver."
}
